<p class="lh">Custom website development pricing varies widely — from lean marketing sites to enterprise portals with authentication, integrations, and ongoing content operations. Understanding what drives cost helps you budget realistically, compare vendor proposals fairly, and avoid surprises after launch.</p>

<h2 id="cost-drivers">What drives website development cost</h2>
<p class="lh">The biggest cost factors are not visual design alone. Discovery depth, number of page templates, CMS complexity, third-party integrations (CRM, payment, analytics), multilingual requirements, and accessibility standards all affect effort. A ten-page brochure site and a ten-page authenticated portal are not the same project.</p>
<p class="lh">Technology stack also matters. WordPress can accelerate content-heavy sites; Laravel or Node.js backends suit custom workflows and API-driven frontends built with React. SanguineIT scopes projects based on user journeys and integration boundaries — see our <a href="custom-web-development.php">custom web development</a> practice for typical engagement patterns.</p>

<h2 id="project-types">Cost ranges by project type</h2>
<p class="lh"><strong>Marketing websites</strong> — Typically the leanest scope: responsive layouts, CMS for blog and landing pages, contact forms, and basic SEO setup. Timeline often runs four to eight weeks for a well-defined brand and content package.</p>
<p class="lh"><strong>Business web applications</strong> — Portals with login, role-based access, dashboards, and ERP or CRM integrations require more architecture, QA, and security work. Budget for discovery, API design, and staged releases rather than a single big-bang launch.</p>
<p class="lh"><strong>Progressive web apps</strong> — Single codebase for web and installable mobile-like experiences. Cost sits between a marketing site and full native apps when offline capability and push notifications are required.</p>

<h2 id="hidden-costs">Costs teams forget to plan for</h2>
<ul class="blog-article-list">
    <li>Hosting, CDN, SSL, and domain management after go-live.</li>
    <li>Content migration, copywriting, and image production if not included in scope.</li>
    <li>Third-party license fees (plugins, fonts, stock assets, SaaS tools).</li>
    <li>Post-launch <a href="website-maintenance.php">website maintenance</a> — security patches, dependency updates, and uptime monitoring.</li>
    <li>Performance tuning and SEO iteration in the first 90 days after launch.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="reduce-spend">How to reduce spend without cutting quality</h2>
<p class="lh">Start with a focused MVP: ship core journeys first, then iterate. Reuse proven design systems instead of custom components for every page. Define integration requirements early so backend work does not get reworked mid-project. Fixed-scope phases with clear acceptance criteria often deliver better value than open-ended hourly engagements without a roadmap.</p>

<h2 id="conclusion">The bottom line</h2>
<p class="lh">Custom website development cost reflects complexity, integrations, and governance — not just page count. Plan for discovery, launch, and maintenance as one lifecycle. <a href="contact-us.php">Contact SanguineIT</a> for a scoped estimate aligned to your goals and timeline.</p>
